export const BUILTIN_PROMPTS = {
  screening: "# Evidence Map — Screening Prompt\n\nHow the screening model judges each abstract found for an evidence map. The server sends it once per article: {{topic}} filled in with the EVID's topic, {{article}} with the article's PMID, title, year, publication types and abstract. With it goes the answer's form, a JSON schema the model must fill — the map's columns, fixed by the server:\n\n- relevance — direct (the topic's intervention itself, any outcome) / indirect (a broader category, a related intervention, or pooled with others) / background (animal, in vitro, mechanism, epidemiology) / off-topic (out of the map)\n- population, design, n — what kind of study it is. These three are the same for every topic: the server stores them with the article in the library (`design`, `participants`, `population`; `characteristics.md` has the definitions and the prompt a plain `fh_library_add` uses for them)\n- population — humans / animals / in vitro / none\n- design — the study design, e.g. meta-analysis, RCT, cohort, case report, animal study\n- n — participants, if the abstract states it\n- outcome — hard (death, incidence, clinical events) / surrogate (biomarker, imaging) / none\n- finding — benefit / no effect / harm / mixed / none: the intervention's effect\n- aspect — efficacy / safety / mechanism / dosing / interaction / other\n- reason — one sentence: what the paper adds to the topic\n\nThis prompt says how to judge; the fields say what to answer. Everything above the line is for people and is not sent.\n\n---\n\nScreen this article for the topic: {{topic}}\n\nRead the abstract and fill every field from it; where the abstract says nothing, answer none.\n\n{{article}}\n",
  ranking: "# Evidence Map — Ranking Prompt\n\nHow the ranking model picks the articles that matter most. Two steps, one section each: **Assign** — which of the map's best articles belong to which aspect, whatever query found them: {{topic}}, {{items}} (the numbered aspects) and {{articles}} (the numbered articles, each with its finding); **Items** — the picks for each aspect (a benefit, a risk, an interaction): {{topic}}, {{picks}} (how many per aspect) and {{groups}} — one group per aspect, its name and its candidates. The answer's form is fixed by the server.\n\nEverything above the line is for people and is not sent.\n\n---\n\n## Assign\n\nA review of the topic \"{{topic}}\" is about to be written. Below are its items — each a benefit, a risk, an interaction or a protocol question of the intervention — and a list of articles found for the topic.\n\nFor each article name the items a review could cite it for:\n- an article belongs to an item when it reports on that item for the topic's intervention: a result, a rate, a dose — also as one outcome among several, as a broad meta-analysis or a large trial does\n- most articles belong to one to three items; an article that fits no item is left out of the answer\n- not by a shared word alone: the article must say something about the item\n\nAnswer with each article's number and the numbers of its items.\n\nItems:\n{{items}}\n\nArticles:\n{{articles}}\n\n## Items\n\nA review of the topic \"{{topic}}\" is about to be written. Below are groups of candidate articles; each group answers one question about the intervention, named in its heading (a benefit, a risk, an interaction, a protocol question, or the intervention's systematic reviews).\n\nThe topic names its goal (\"… for Health & Longevity\": prevention and long-term outcomes in people who use the intervention for that). Where a group's candidates allow a choice, the articles that serve that goal come first.\n\nFor each group pick the articles the review needs for that question, the most important first — up to {{picks}}, or the number its heading names:\n- for a benefit: the best systematic review or meta-analysis (the newest or largest), then the contrary or null evidence, then the key trial\n- for a risk or an interaction: the human studies that document it — the review, the trial, the pharmacokinetic study, the case report where nothing else exists\n- for a modifying factor (genetic variants, baseline biomarkers, sex, pre-existing conditions, age): the articles that report how the factor changes the benefit or the harm — not one that only measured the factor or had it among its participants\n- for a group named \"overall\" (safety, interactions, pharmacokinetics): the broad reviews and the largest studies of that side of the intervention as a whole — a study of one drug or one effect belongs to its own group, not here; no pick where no such overview exists\n- for a protocol question: the study or guideline a dose or a pharmacokinetic figure can be cited to, for the use the topic's goal means — for health and longevity the long-term use by people who take it for that (for a supplement: oral, at the usual doses), not the dosing of acute or intensive care\n- for the group of the intervention's systematic reviews: only systematic reviews and meta-analyses (no single trial, no cohort or Mendelian randomization study, no narrative review) — the major ones for the goal the topic names, by citations, size and recency, spread over its main questions rather than several on one; a review of the intervention itself before one of its compound class; a topic on health and longevity is about prevention and long-term outcomes in people who use the intervention for that — not about its use in acute or intensive care, which gets at most one pick; where the intervention involves a trade-off, at least one on the claimed effect and one on the principal risk\n- in every group: a study in humans before one in animals or in vitro, which is picked only where the group has no human study that answers its question; a letter, comment or editorial only where nothing else does\n- only articles that are about the group's question and study the topic's intervention itself; a group whose candidates do not answer its question gets no pick\n\nAnswer with each group's number and the numbers of its picked candidates.\n\n{{groups}}\n",
  discovery: "# Evidence Map — Discovery Prompt\n\nHow the discovery model finds the articles for a map. The server sends one section per step. **Survey** — the queries for a first look at what the intervention has been studied for. Then four lists, each from the titles the survey found, each item with its own query: **Benefits**, **Risks**, **Interactions**, **Protocol** — the tables an evidence review has (its fifth, the major systematic reviews, comes from the survey itself). Each item names its group inside the table — the review's sections: a benefit is an outcome or a modifying factor; a risk an adverse effect or a modifying factor; an interaction a drug, a supplement or food, or a population to avoid; a protocol item dosing, pharmacokinetics, a response modifier, or discontinuation and cycling. **Tables** — read by the server, never sent: the sections the map answers by (the sections of the review to be written), the lists their aspects come from, and which survey queries feed them; another review structure is other rows there and a list section for each list. **Iteration** — a survey query with more hits than are read is split or narrowed (only when the survey is read whole). **Empty** — a survey query that found nothing is written once more. **Hints** is put into each step in place of {{hints}}. Slots: {{topic}}; {{titles}} and {{max}} (the lists: at most this many items); {{queries}} and {{limit}} (Iteration). The answers' form is fixed by the server.\n\nEverything above the line is never sent.\n\n---\n\n## Tables\n\n<!-- Read by the server, never sent to a model: what the map is made of. A map answers by section — the sections of the review that is to be written — and a section holds aspects, each with its articles.\nSurvey query: a kind of query the Survey step writes. Read: \"all\" — its whole result; \"first\" — its first results. Aspect, Section: the aspect its results stand for and that aspect's section (none: its articles are in the map, under no aspect).\nList: a section of this prompt that writes a list of aspects. Max aspects: its ceiling. Titles from: the survey queries whose titles it reads first. Screening label: the screening's label its other titles are sorted by.\nSection: a section of the review. List: the list its aspects come from (none: the section is its survey aspect alone). Groups: the groups of that list that belong to the section, separated by \";\" — the list's model names each aspect's group. Picks: articles picked per aspect (none: the server's default). -->\n\n| Survey query | Read | Aspect | Section |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| reviews | first | Systematic reviews and meta-analyses of the intervention | Systematic Reviews |\n| safety | first | Safety overall | Potential Risks & Side Effects |\n| interactions | all | Interactions overall | Key Interactions & Contraindications |\n| pharmacokinetics | first | Pharmacokinetics overall | Therapeutic Protocol |\n| guidelines | first | Guidelines | Therapeutic Protocol |\n\n| List | Max aspects | Titles from | Screening label |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Benefits | 80 | reviews | efficacy |\n| Risks | 40 | safety | safety |\n| Interactions | 40 | interactions | interaction |\n| Protocol | 15 | pharmacokinetics, guidelines | dosing |\n\n| Section | List | Groups | Picks |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Systematic Reviews | — | — | 5 |\n| Expected Benefits | Benefits | outcome | — |\n| Benefit-Modifying Factors | Benefits | modifying factor | — |\n| Potential Risks & Side Effects | Risks | adverse effect | — |\n| Risk-Modifying Factors | Risks | modifying factor | — |\n| Key Interactions & Contraindications | Interactions | drug; supplement or food; population to avoid | — |\n| Therapeutic Protocol | Protocol | dosing; pharmacokinetics; response modifier | — |\n| Discontinuation & Cycling | Protocol | discontinuation and cycling | — |\n\n## Survey\n\nWrite the PubMed queries for a first survey of this topic: {{topic}}\n\nThe survey is to show what the intervention has been studied for — every benefit, every risk, every interaction — so that a second round can ask about each one by name. It reads the reviews, not the single studies. Queries that usually serve:\n- systematic reviews and meta-analyses of the intervention — and those of its compound class, which cover it under the class's name (\"NAD+ precursors\" for nicotinamide riboside): the intervention's names and the class's in title or abstract ([tiab])\n- narrative reviews of its effects and uses\n- its safety: reviews of adverse effects and toxicity, case reports of rare harms\n- its interactions with drugs, supplements and foods\n- its pharmacokinetics\n- guidelines and position statements\n- for a drug, safety across its class; for a combination, each part on its own\n\nAbout 6 to 10 queries, each with its kind and a short label. Only the first results of each are read, so one query per question, the names in the title. The interactions query is read whole, so it must hold the human interaction and pharmacokinetic studies and little else — a few hundred results, not thousands: the names in the title, the words of such a study (pharmacokinetics, bioavailability, absorption, interaction), humans, and a study design. E.g. (centrophenoxine[ti] OR meclofenoxate[ti]) AND (pharmacokinetic*[tiab] OR bioavailability[tiab] OR absorption[tiab] OR \"drug interactions\"[mh] OR interaction*[ti]) AND humans[mh] AND (clinical trial[pt] OR randomized controlled trial[pt] OR crossover[tiab] OR volunteers[tiab])\n\n{{hints}}\n\n## Benefits\n\nTopic: {{topic}}\n\nA review of this topic has a table of benefits: every benefit the intervention has been studied for, each backed by its evidence. Below are the titles of the articles a survey found. List the benefits, one item each, in two groups:\n- outcome: every distinct outcome or condition the intervention has been studied for, whatever the result — also where it did not help. The goal's own outcomes (for longevity: aging and lifespan as one item, also where only animals have been studied, then e.g. mortality, frailty and physical function) and the intervention's main medical use belong in the list also when the titles lack them. An outcome far from the topic's goal (a rare disease, a use in children or in intensive care) only where a review or a trial exists for it.\n- modifying factor: what changes the benefit, where it has been studied — genetic variants, baseline biomarker levels, sex, pre-existing conditions, age. One item per factor, at most these five.\n\nKeep distinct outcomes apart — cataract and macular degeneration are two items, blood glucose and metabolic syndrome are two.\n\nThe number is a ceiling, not a target: at most {{max}} items, and fewer where less has been studied — never fill the list. Only items of this table: nothing that belongs to another table of the review (benefits, risks, interactions, protocol), and no item that only names a kind of article (\"reviews\", \"safety\", \"pharmacokinetics\"). No item twice: not the same thing at another granularity, split by population or endpoint, or as a narrower form of an item already listed, unless it has been studied on its own.\n\nEach item names its group.\n\n{{item_query}}\n\n{{hints}}\n\nTitles:\n\n{{titles}}\n\n## Risks\n\nTopic: {{topic}}\n\nA review of this topic has a table of risks: every harm the intervention can do, each backed by its evidence. Below are the titles of the articles a survey found. List the risks, one item each, in two groups:\n- adverse effect: every adverse effect reported, the common and the rare ones — a rare harm known from case reports is an item too; toxicity and overdose; effects on laboratory tests. A known adverse effect of the intervention or of its class that the titles lack belongs in the list too. An outcome that a study found worsened by the intervention is an adverse effect too, also where it was studied as a benefit (a disease that progressed faster, a performance that fell): a title marked \"found harm\" is such a study.\n- modifying factor: what changes the risk, where it has been studied — genetic variants, baseline biomarker levels, sex, pre-existing conditions, age. One item per factor, at most these five.\n\nNot here: a drug, supplement or food taken with the intervention, and a group of people who should not take it at all — both are in the table of interactions.\n\nKeep distinct harms apart.\n\nThe number is a ceiling, not a target: at most {{max}} items, and fewer where less has been studied — never fill the list. Only items of this table: nothing that belongs to another table of the review (benefits, risks, interactions, protocol), and no item that only names a kind of article (\"reviews\", \"safety\", \"pharmacokinetics\"). No item twice: not the same thing at another granularity, split by population or endpoint, or as a narrower form of an item already listed, unless it has been studied on its own.\n\nEach item names its group.\n\n{{item_query}}\n\n{{hints}}\n\nThe titles of the survey's safety search come first: every adverse effect named in one of them is a risk item.\n\nTitles:\n\n{{titles}}\n\n## Interactions\n\nTopic: {{topic}}\n\nA review of this topic has a table of interactions and contraindications, each backed by its evidence. Below are the titles of the articles a survey found. List them, one item each, in three groups:\n- drug: each prescription or over-the-counter drug on its own — one item per substance, never several in one item; a single human study of the interaction of one substance with the intervention is enough to make it an item. A drug class as an item where the interaction is known for the class (e.g. diuretics, proton pump inhibitors).\n- supplement or food: each supplement or food on its own, also one with an additive effect (e.g. another supplement that lowers blood pressure, for an antihypertensive).\n- population to avoid: each group of people who should not use the intervention — pregnancy, breastfeeding, children, a disease, an organ impairment, a genetic trait — where harm in that group has been studied or reported. The group is defined by what people are or have before they take it; a condition the intervention was reported to cause or worsen is an adverse effect and belongs to the risks, not here.\n\nAn interaction is what the two do to each other in people: a change in the blood level or the effect of either, or a harm of the pair. A substance that was only given together with the intervention is no item: not the partner of a combination therapy or a fixed-dose combination studied for its efficacy, and not a substance tested with the intervention in animals or in vitro only.\n\nA well-known interaction or contraindication of the intervention (one a drug label or a guideline names) that the titles lack belongs in the list too.\n\nThe number is a ceiling, not a target: at most {{max}} items, and fewer where less has been studied — never fill the list. Only items of this table: nothing that belongs to another table of the review (benefits, risks, interactions, protocol), and no item that only names a kind of article (\"reviews\", \"safety\", \"pharmacokinetics\"). No item twice: not the same thing at another granularity, split by population or endpoint, or as a narrower form of an item already listed, unless it has been studied on its own.\n\nEach item names its group.\n\n{{item_query}}\n\n{{hints}}\n\nThe titles of the survey's interactions search come first: every drug, supplement or food whose interaction with the intervention one of them studies is an item; so is every group of people it was found harmful for.\n\nTitles:\n\n{{titles}}\n\n## Protocol\n\nTopic: {{topic}}\n\nA review of this topic describes how the intervention is used — its therapeutic protocol — and cites each dose to a source. Below are the titles of the articles a survey found. List what the protocol needs, one item each, in four groups:\n- dosing: the doses and regimens used in trials and named in guidelines; dose-response; a single dose against split doses; the time of day; the forms and routes that differ in effect (e.g. oral against intravenous, one salt or formulation against another)\n- pharmacokinetics: half-life, bioavailability, absorption\n- response modifier: what changes the dose or the response — genetic variants, baseline biomarker levels, sex, pre-existing conditions, age\n- discontinuation and cycling: long-term against short-term use, withdrawal and rebound, tapering, cycling\n\nThe protocol is for the goal the topic names: for health and longevity the long-term use by people who take the intervention for that — not the dosing of acute or intensive care, which is at most one item (the routes compared).\n\nOnly what has been studied for this intervention.\n\nThe number is a ceiling, not a target: at most {{max}} items, and fewer where less has been studied — never fill the list. Only items of this table: nothing that belongs to another table of the review (benefits, risks, interactions, protocol), and no item that only names a kind of article (\"reviews\", \"safety\", \"pharmacokinetics\"). No item twice: not the same thing at another granularity, split by population or endpoint, or as a narrower form of an item already listed, unless it has been studied on its own.\n\nEach item names its group.\n\n{{item_query}}\n\n{{hints}}\n\nTitles:\n\n{{titles}}\n\n## Item query\n\nFor each item write one PubMed query that finds the studies of the intervention on that item. Only the first results of each query are read, so it has to be narrow: the intervention's names in the title ([ti]) and the item's own words in the title too, with their synonyms. One item per query.\n\n## Iteration\n\nTopic: {{topic}}\n\nEach of these PubMed queries has more than {{limit}} hits; only the first {{limit}} of a query are read, so the rest would be lost. Replace each one by one or more queries that each stay under {{limit}} hits and together keep what the query was for:\n- the intervention's names in the title ([ti])\n- split by outcome, by study design (reviews; trials; observational studies) or by population\n- the question's words in the title where title and abstract give too many\n\nNot by publication date. A query that cannot be narrowed without losing its purpose: give it back unchanged.\n\n{{hints}}\n\nQueries (number, hits, query):\n\n{{queries}}\n\n## Empty\n\nTopic: {{topic}}\n\nEach of these survey queries found nothing on PubMed. Write each one again so that it finds what it was for:\n- the names in title or abstract ([tiab]) instead of the title alone; fewer names rather than more — a brand name nobody writes in a title adds nothing\n- for systematic reviews, also the name of the intervention's compound class\n- brackets around the names joined by OR\n\nOne query for each, with its number. A query that is right as it is — the literature has nothing — is given back unchanged.\n\n{{hints}}\n\nQueries (number, query):\n\n{{queries}}\n\n## Hints\n\nHints for writing the queries:\n- every name the intervention goes by: the generic name, brand and trade names (also former and foreign ones), chemical names, common abbreviations\n- only names of the intervention itself: not the food or plant it comes from, its precursor or its compound class (urolithin A is not pomegranate or ellagitannins) — a study of those is not a study of the intervention. The one exception: the survey's query for systematic reviews also names the compound class, never the food or plant\n- names joined by OR stand in brackets of their own before an AND: (a[ti] OR b[ti]) AND c[ti] — without them PubMed reads left to right\n- names untagged let PubMed also find them by their MeSH term; in the title ([ti]) they keep the articles that are about the intervention, where untagged names bring thousands that only mention it\n- reviews and meta-analyses of the intervention name it in their title\n- tags fit the question words: [tiab] for title and abstract, [pt] for publication types, * for word endings\n- filters on MeSH terms or publication types miss articles not yet indexed, often the newest; title words beside them keep those\n- e.g. (centrophenoxine[ti] OR meclofenoxate[ti] OR lucidril[ti]) AND (systematic review[pt] OR meta-analysis[pt] OR systematic review[ti] OR meta-analy*[ti])\n- e.g. (centrophenoxine OR meclofenoxate OR lucidril) AND (interaction*[tiab] OR coadministration[tiab] OR concomitant[tiab])\n",
  characteristics: "# Study Characteristics Prompt\n\nHow a model reads what kind of study an article is, from its abstract: the design, the number of participants, and whether it was done in humans, animals or in vitro. The server sends it once per article — {{article}} filled in with the title and abstract — when an article is added to the library (`fh_library_add`) and a screening model is configured; the answer is stored with the article and returned with its metadata (`fh_library_status`, `fh_pubmed_fetch_articles`): `design`, `participants`, `population`, and `characteristics_by` — the model that read them.\n\nThese are the same for every topic, so they belong to the article, not to an evidence map. A map's screening reads them in the same call as its own judgment and stores them the same way, with the same definitions; an article that already has them keeps them.\n\nThe answer's fields are fixed by the server:\n- design — the study design, e.g. meta-analysis, systematic review, RCT, cohort, case-control, case report, review, animal study, in vitro\n- n — the participants, if the abstract states it (stored as `participants`)\n- population — humans / animals / in vitro / none\n\nEverything above the line is never sent.\n\n---\n\nRead this article's title and abstract and state what kind of study it is: its design, the number of participants if the abstract states it, and whether it was done in humans, animals or in vitro (none: no study population, e.g. a commentary or a methods paper).\n\n{{article}}\n",
} as const;
